{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310767,"nonce":"8GAdCL+RXieTHqXLJKX1OfcV6Mo=","title":"Phase (b) of the f64-hardening plan: shipped `rayzor.Double` as a documentation typedef over Float, and fixed the `Array\u003cFloat\u003e.toString` ...","message":"`compiler/haxe-std/rayzor/Double.hx`:\n\n```haxe\npackage rayzor;\ntypedef Double = Float;\n```\n\nToday this is a transparent type alias. Users adopt `Double` to *document*\nintent (\"I rely on f64 round-trip exactness\") at API boundaries — model\nweights, audio buffers, scientific data. Behaviour identical to `Float`\nincluding the f64-precision fixes that landed in `bugs_array_map_float_audit`.\n\nFuture promotion to a nominally-distinct abstract is blocked by:\n\n\n```haxe\nabstract Double(Float) from Float to Float {}\nvar d:Double = 3.14;\ntrace(\"d=\" + d);     // → \"d=3\"   ❌ should be 3.14\n```\n\nEven the bare `abstract Double(Float) from Float to Float {}` truncates\nthe f64 value to int. Float→3.14 goes in, Double→3 comes out. Affects\nboth the abstract storage layer and the `@:from Float` conversion path.\n\nReproduction: any `abstract X(Float) from Float to Float`-shape declaration\nwill hit this. SIMD4f's `@:coreType extern abstract` pattern works\nbecause the compiler treats `@:coreType` abstracts as primitives (no\nunderlying type), so the broken transparent-abstract layer isn't\ninvolved.\n\nThis blocks promoting `Double` to a nominally distinct type via the\nstraightforward Haxe abstract idiom. Workarounds for a nominal Double:\n- `@:coreType extern abstract Double` like SIMD4f, with compiler-side\n  recognition of \"rayzor.Double\" → `IrType::F64` (significant integration\n  work — touches ~10 places per the SIMD4f grep results).\n- Fix the abstract-over-Float lowering — narrow root-cause fix but\n  unknown effort until investigated.\n\nDocumented as a follow-up for a future session.\n\n\n`format_array_slot` in `runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs` now runs an f64\nplausibility check *before* the pointer-alignment gate. The previous order\nmisclassified 3.14's bit pattern (0x400921FB54442D1F, low 3 bits = 0b111)\nas a raw int because the `\u0026 0x7 != 0` check fired first.\n\nThe new order:\n\n1. Null → \"null\"\n2. Small int (\u003c 4 GiB) → raw int\n3. **F64 plausibility**: `as_f64` is finite and `|as_f64| ∈ [1e-10, 1e15]`\n   → format as f64. (Heap pointers reinterpreted as f64 give subnormals\n   ~10⁻³⁰⁸, far below this window — they wouldn't pass.)\n4. Pointer-alignment gate (low 3 bits zero, in user-heap range)\n5. Probe DynamicValue header, else raw int\n\nVerified:\n\n| input | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `[3.14, 2.718, 1.414]` | `[4614253070214989087, 4613302810693613912, 4609046909045748793]` | `[3.14, 2.718, 1.414]` ✓ |\n| `[3.14, -1.5, 0.001, 1e10]` | nonsense | `[3.14, -1.5, 0.001, 10000000000]` ✓ |\n| `[1, 42, -5, 1000, 1000000]` | unchanged | `[1, 42, -5, 1000, 1000000]` ✓ |\n| `[\"hello\", \"world\"]` | unchanged | `[hello, world]` ✓ |\n\nCompanion helper `format_f64_for_string`:\n- NaN/Infinity → \"NaN\", \"Infinity\", \"-Infinity\"\n- Integer-valued f64 (e.g. 3.0) → \"3\" (Haxe convention)\n- Otherwise → standard f64 formatter\n\nThe same helper backs a new `haxe_array_to_string_f64` runtime function\ndeclared but not yet wired through a dispatcher — left as the cleaner\ntype-driven path for when somebody wires `Array\u003cFloat\u003e.toString` to\ndispatch on element type. Today the heuristic carries the load.\n\n\n- A `build_array_to_string` wrapper variant taking an `elem_type_tag: I32`\n  param. The compile-site dispatch (injecting the tag based on receiver's\n  type_args) was significant scope creep for marginal value over the\n  heuristic. Saved the runtime function (`haxe_array_to_string_f64`) and\n  its extern declaration in case a future PR wants to wire it up.\n\n\n128/128 haxe + 709 lib + 78 runtime + tiny-transformer all green.\n\n\n- [[bugs-array-map-float-audit]] — Phase (a) fixes that made `Array\u003cFloat\u003e`\n  / `Map\u003cK,Float\u003e` actually correct. The stringifier was the last\n  user-visible f64 bug from that audit.\n- [[bugs-gguf-q6k-and-f32-load]] — the original session that surfaced\n  the whole f64-precision class.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `feature_double_and_array_stringifier` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}